course: dictionary
lesson: 7138
title: Pension: money for later
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pension is money paid regularly to someone who has stopped working, usually because they have retired. «Grandma lives on her pension now that she has retired.» The clue is retired: she no longer goes to work, so the money comes to her every week or month.
explain: A pension can also be paid to someone who is ill, disabled or widowed. Widowed means a husband or wife has died. The meaning stays the same: regular money to live on. Look at the other words in the sentence to see which meaning fits.
explain: Money saved during working life, often with the employer adding some, is also called a pension. «Dad pays a little into his pension each month, and his work adds more.» The big idea is saving now for later.
explain: In some parts of Europe, a pension (said «pon-see-on») is a small family hotel. As a verb, to pension someone off means to make them retire. If the sentence is about a hotel, a room or a holiday, it is the hotel meaning.
fact: A pension is money paid regularly to live on, often after you retire.
fact: A pensioner is a person who gets a pension.
fact: «Pension» money saved for old age can be put into shares, so its value can go up or down.
fact: Yearly pension = monthly pension × 12. A pension of £400 a month is £4,800 a year.
fact: Phrases to know: state pension, pension scheme, draw a pension.
exercise: «Grandad stopped work last year and now gets a pension every month.» What does pension mean here? | A kind of car | A small hotel by the sea | A school playground | *Money paid regularly after retiring
exercise: Maya says only people who are working can get a pension. What is the true answer? | Yes, only people who are at work | Yes, but only if they are under ten | *No, people who are ill, disabled or widowed can get one too | No, pensions are only paid in hotels
exercise: «The company decided to pension off its oldest workers.» What does pension off mean? | To move them to a new office | To pay them more for working | *To make them retire | To give them a holiday
test: What does pension mean in «We stayed at a pension in Italy, a small family hotel with six rooms»? | A kind of shares | Money paid after retiring | *A small family hotel | A cheap car
test: «Money saved in a pension is put into shares, so it can rise or fall.» Why can a pension go down in value? | Because the bank closes on Friday | *Because shares can fall in price | The money is paid out every week | Because it is a hotel
exam: A pension is £400 a month. How much is it for a whole year? | £480 | *£4,800 | £40,000 | £4,000
